  "slug": "learning-quantum-40ee",
  "summary": "Recent preprints target noise and exponential concentration in parametrized quantum models with boosting, reusable calibration and training-free feature maps.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is keeping quantum machine learning trainable under hardware noise and exponential concentration. One route replaces or supplements error mitigation. Boosting of noisy parametrized circuits is compared against probabilistic error cancellation and found to need fewer resources. Bagging and AdaBoost are applied to variational regressors. A reusable calibration framework restores QNN convergence. A learned small-circuit mitigator combined with circuit cutting outperforms ZNE and Clifford-based methods on larger circuits. A second route limits variational optimization: Soft-MoQE classically gates fixed quantum feature maps and matches trainable QNNs on MNIST. Riemannian-gradient schemes avoid measurement and classical feedback, and a quantum Transformer is trained via multi-agent RL without backpropagation. An empirical study links entanglement, locality and bandwidth scaling to concentration in quantum kernels and extreme learning. Contractive layers with one trainable parameter add expressivity on Rydberg arrays. Applications include end-to-end b/c-jet tagging on a 5-qubit superconducting processor and Dempster-Shafer uncertainty-aware classification. All works are preprints.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
